  "text": "Who could fall for some silly scam like the Nigerian Prince Fraud? Well actually, some Nigerian politicians. You see, when these, help me now and I'll give you 10 times as much later, scams started to flood email inboxes in the early 2000s, it cost almost a billion dollars in victim losses worldwide. To combat this, the Nigerian government formed an Economic and Financial Crimes Commission, or EFCC, which helped victims of this advanced fee type scam get some of their money back. However, this new commission completely backfired. Since corruption was so widespread in Nigeria, the scammers did something genius. Instead of targeting random people abroad, they decided to attack Nigerian politicians. Scammers would disguise themselves as EFCC agents and would send letters to government officials saying that they know about the bribes they have taken, but if the politicians sent them some money, they would stop the investigation and would leave them alone. Though these emails were suspicious, the politicians that were indeed shady would comply with the scammers out of desperation and paranoia, letting fraudsters rake in millions of dollars even though they never truly knew which politicians were actually crooked."
